title op3: a recreated directory move, carrying an edit

# The fullness rule of Definition 16.3 in its smallest honest form,
# and the first fixture where the vote triad does work red cannot.
#
# Off-of moves /old to /new the way a tool that rebuilds rather than
# renames does it -- cp -r then rm -r -- so the directory and both
# files get fresh dnodes.  Then keep.txt is edited.  Onto is quiet.
#
# Red sees nothing: every dnode below /new is new.  Yellow refuses to
# compare directories at all (Definition 2.8), so today this decays
# to a death and a birth -- /old and its two files deleted, /new and
# its two files created.  Clean, but every output object is
# materialized, and onto-side handles and links do not survive it.
#
# The children vote instead.
#
# PHASE 1 has exactly one route to this candidate, which is worth
# being precise about because an earlier draft of the theory had two.
# There is no red anchor here (nothing below /new kept a dnode), and
# yellow deliberately does not nominate -- a nomination is the
# inverted question "what, anywhere, is yellow to this child," which
# is a content index the pairwise yellow oracle is designed not to
# be.  So (base /old, off-of /new) reaches the docket only as a
# deletion and an addition among the siblings of the root, which is
# corresponded by green.  The sibling generator carries this fixture
# by itself.
#
# PHASE 2 judges at height 1.  Both children vote into /new by NAME
# MATCH -- the same leaf inside the candidate pair.  That is the
# relativized-green row of the triad, and the row is why an edit does
# not silence a voter: a name match survives an edit and survives a
# dnode sever, and keep.txt suffered both at once.  Every voter votes
# into /new, the votes are one-to-one, /new is the unique target,
# there are no emigrants and no abstentions.  The move is believed.
#
# Hand-worked: clean, and -- the whole point -- the output reuses
# onto's dnodes 2, 3 and 4 rather than minting 10-300, 11-300 and
# 12-300.  The VERDICT is clean either way; it is REALIZATION that
# differs, which this suite's gold checks last and separately.  A
# fixture whose old and new answers differ only in dnode identity is
# the easiest kind to get wrong by eye, and the hardest to get wrong
# by gold.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: old/
      3-100: keep.txt = kv0
      4-100: other.txt = ov0

tree onto
  1-1: /
    2-100: old/
      3-100: keep.txt = kv0
      4-100: other.txt = ov0

tree offof
  1-1: /
    10-300: new/
      11-300: keep.txt = kv1
      12-300: other.txt = ov0

#
# WORKED OUTCOME (by hand, before any implementation ran)
#   believed moves:  /old -> /new, on both children voting by name
#                    match.  Nominated by the sibling generator alone
#   deltas:          none.  Relative to the believed pair both leaves
#                    are unchanged
#   decisions:       the directory renames old to new reusing onto's
#                    dnode 2; keep.txt takes off-of's bytes reusing
#                    dnode 3; other.txt keeps, reusing dnode 4.
#                    Without the belief all three materialize, which
#                    is the only difference the gold can see
#   conflicts:       none, onto is quiet
#   emission:        one directory rename, and one write to keep.txt
#                    inside its scope
# --- expected result, worked by hand 2026-08-31 ---
expect clean

tree expected
  1-1: /
    2-100: new
      3-100: keep.txt = kv1
      4-100: other.txt = ov0
